WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.236
This is the technical deep
dive for Book 04 of the

2
00:00:03.236 --> 00:00:06.900
Federal Modernization
Workbook, Access and Privilege.

3
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.588
The fifteen-minute briefing
made the argument.

4
00:00:09.588 --> 00:00:13.860
This session is for the people who
build and run it: identity engineers,

5
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:18.500
architects, and the security and assessment
staff who have to prove it works.

6
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.290
It runs about fifty
minutes in five modules,

7
00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:25.088
and each module stands on its own,
so you can watch it in parts.

8
00:00:25.088 --> 00:00:30.432
After a two-slide orientation, we'll cover
the Conditional Access policy library;

9
00:00:30.432 --> 00:00:35.778
its rollout, exclusions and break-glass
accounts; PIM self-request with approval;

10
00:00:35.778 --> 00:00:40.724
access governance; and finally access
as code, and the evidence it produces.

11
00:00:40.724 --> 00:00:44.056
Every fact comes from a
footnoted chapter of the book,

12
00:00:44.056 --> 00:00:46.100
and each slide names its chapter.

13
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.440
Code and JSON stay in the book;
the slides carry the design.

14
00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:53.768
Start with why, because
every design choice

15
00:00:53.768 --> 00:00:56.490
in the next forty-five
minutes follows from it.

16
00:00:56.490 --> 00:01:02.049
OMB M-22-09 says federal applications
can't rely on the network perimeter,

17
00:01:02.049 --> 00:01:05.572
and that every request for
access should be evaluated,

18
00:01:05.572 --> 00:01:09.565
with the ability to evaluate an
active session continuously.

19
00:01:09.565 --> 00:01:11.255
When undue risk appears,

20
00:01:11.255 --> 00:01:15.565
the answer can be re-authentication,
limited access or denial.

21
00:01:15.565 --> 00:01:19.924
It also asks agencies to move past
static roles toward more granular,

22
00:01:19.924 --> 00:01:25.278
dynamic permissions, and to use at least
one device-level signal alongside identity.

23
00:01:25.278 --> 00:01:29.815
NIST SP 800-207 gives the architecture.

24
00:01:29.815 --> 00:01:35.136
Three of its tenets carry this book:
network location alone doesn't imply trust;

25
00:01:35.136 --> 00:01:38.655
access is granted per session,
with least privilege;

26
00:01:38.655 --> 00:01:41.602
and access is determined
by dynamic policy.

27
00:01:41.602 --> 00:01:44.189
It also separates deciding from enforcing.

28
00:01:44.189 --> 00:01:48.868
A policy decision point, made of a policy
engine and a policy administrator,

29
00:01:48.868 --> 00:01:52.565
decides; policy enforcement
points carry the decision out.

30
00:01:52.565 --> 00:01:55.927
The table is how the program
maps that onto Entra ID.

31
00:01:55.927 --> 00:01:58.477
Conditional Access is the policy engine.

32
00:01:58.477 --> 00:02:02.645
Entra ID's token issuance
plays the policy administrator:

33
00:02:02.645 --> 00:02:06.465
it issues the token the
decision allows, or it refuses.

34
00:02:06.465 --> 00:02:09.904
The applications that accept
only Entra-issued tokens,

35
00:02:09.904 --> 00:02:14.615
and the gateways in front of older
applications, are the enforcement points.

36
00:02:14.615 --> 00:02:20.615
And Continuous Access Evaluation answers
M-22-09's active-session requirement,

37
00:02:20.615 --> 00:02:24.510
re-evaluating on critical events,
such as a disabled account,

38
00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:28.640
a password reset or token revocation,
and when location changes.

39
00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.065
Be careful how you state that
mapping in a security plan.

40
00:02:32.065 --> 00:02:37.015
It's the program's interpretation, not
a mapping Microsoft or NIST publishes.

41
00:02:37.015 --> 00:02:42.409
But it explains why the rest of this session
treats a Conditional Access policy as

42
00:02:42.409 --> 00:02:44.153
the unit of access control:

43
00:02:44.153 --> 00:02:48.150
one policy set the agency can
read, test, change and prove.

44
00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:53.273
Now the mechanism, because Conditional
Access doesn't work like Group Policy,

45
00:02:53.273 --> 00:02:57.988
and administrators who bring Group Policy
habits to it make predictable mistakes.

46
00:02:57.988 --> 00:02:59.400
There's no precedence.

47
00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:02.374
Within a policy, assignments
combine with AND:

48
00:03:02.374 --> 00:03:06.600
the policy applies only when
everything it's assigned to matches.

49
00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:10.825
Across policies, every applicable
policy must be satisfied.

50
00:03:10.825 --> 00:03:14.539
If one requires MFA and
another a compliant device,

51
00:03:14.539 --> 00:03:19.225
the user does both, and the policy
numbers are labels, not an order.

52
00:03:19.225 --> 00:03:23.813
Block wins: if any applicable
policy blocks, enforcement stops.

53
00:03:23.813 --> 00:03:29.637
Inside a policy, controls combine as
configured, require all or require one.

54
00:03:29.637 --> 00:03:31.551
And exclusions are absolute.

55
00:03:31.551 --> 00:03:34.625
The rule that causes the
most damage is the default.

56
00:03:34.625 --> 00:03:38.763
If no policy's conditions trigger
a control, a token is issued.

57
00:03:38.763 --> 00:03:44.066
A policy that says Finance users reaching
Payroll must use phishing-resistant MFA

58
00:03:44.066 --> 00:03:46.065
says nothing about anyone else,

59
00:03:46.065 --> 00:03:50.138
so a non-Finance user gets a Payroll
token with no controls at all.

60
00:03:50.138 --> 00:03:55.111
Restricting a resource takes two
policies, written and reviewed together:

61
00:03:55.111 --> 00:03:58.800
a grant for the population,
and a block for everyone else.

62
00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:02.821
Enterprise application assignment,
with Assignment required set,

63
00:04:02.821 --> 00:04:07.057
is usually the cleaner first gate,
but Global Administrators bypass it,

64
00:04:07.057 --> 00:04:10.575
which is why sensitive applications
keep the block as well.

65
00:04:10.575 --> 00:04:13.113
Two timing facts keep our claims honest.

66
00:04:13.113 --> 00:04:16.706
Conditional Access runs after
first-factor authentication,

67
00:04:16.706 --> 00:04:19.876
so it isn't a front-line
defense against password spray.

68
00:04:19.876 --> 00:04:24.089
And it evaluates token requests:
at sign-in, and when a refresh

69
00:04:24.089 --> 00:04:28.725
token gets a new access token, about
once an hour for modern clients.

70
00:04:28.725 --> 00:04:32.081
Continuous Access Evaluation
closes part of that gap,

71
00:04:32.081 --> 00:04:37.375
and supporting services enforce critical
events typically within fifteen minutes.

72
00:04:37.375 --> 00:04:40.650
With that model in
place, on to module one.

73
00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.900
Module one: the Conditional
Access policy library.

74
00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:48.654
The chapter sets a clear standard
for when a library is finished:

75
00:04:48.654 --> 00:04:52.125
every sign-in in the tenant
meets a policy that someone owns,

76
00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:55.525
and nothing in it depends on
another policy running first.

77
00:04:55.525 --> 00:05:00.780
In the next four slides we'll cover how
the library is numbered and what it assumes

78
00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:06.191
already exists; the personas that every
policy targets; the five enforcement waves,

79
00:05:06.191 --> 00:05:09.093
policy by policy; and
how the library answers

80
00:05:09.093 --> 00:05:13.250
the configurations that
CISA's BOD 25-01 requires.

81
00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:18.080
The deployable JSON template for
every policy is in Appendix A.

82
00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:22.268
The library is one policy
set, numbered once.

83
00:05:22.268 --> 00:05:28.044
Policies CA001 to CA017 are the baseline
that the Federal Modernization Workbook's

84
00:05:28.044 --> 00:05:33.079
Book 02 builds during the migration,
and they keep their identifiers here.

85
00:05:33.079 --> 00:05:37.405
CA002 stays retired, so
that's sixteen policies.

86
00:05:37.405 --> 00:05:43.742
Book 04 adds CA018 to CA038, for risk,
privileged workstations, segmented

87
00:05:43.742 --> 00:05:51.030
applications and workload identities, plus
CA090, the permanent report-only probe.

88
00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:59.055
Numbers 001 to 089 are for the library and
090 to 099 for report-only instruments.

89
00:05:59.055 --> 00:06:03.893
Numbers are labels, not order, and
a retired number is never reused.

90
00:06:03.893 --> 00:06:07.057
The earlier library used a
different numbering scheme,

91
00:06:07.057 --> 00:06:10.567
and the chapter maps every one of
its policies to where it went.

92
00:06:10.567 --> 00:06:13.490
Every name follows one pattern: number,

93
00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:17.143
population, resource or
condition, then control.

94
00:06:17.143 --> 00:06:19.954
You can read a policy on
a call without opening it.

95
00:06:19.954 --> 00:06:23.574
On the right are the prerequisites,
and missing ones are the most

96
00:06:23.574 --> 00:06:27.330
common reason a report-only policy
shows failures that aren't real.

97
00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:29.155
The emergency access group.

98
00:06:29.155 --> 00:06:32.345
Persona groups, the two
big ones dynamic from

99
00:06:32.345 --> 00:06:35.793
extension attributes in
Book 03's attribute map.

100
00:06:35.793 --> 00:06:38.493
Access groups for restricted populations.

101
00:06:38.493 --> 00:06:40.143
Three named locations.

102
00:06:40.143 --> 00:06:43.984
A custom authentication strength
that accepts FIDO2 keys from

103
00:06:43.984 --> 00:06:47.617
an approved AAGUID list, or
certificate-based authentication.

104
00:06:47.617 --> 00:06:52.137
It leaves out Windows Hello for Business
on purpose: for privileged accounts,

105
00:06:52.137 --> 00:06:56.868
a credential held in the device gaining
access doesn't count as a separate device.

106
00:06:56.868 --> 00:06:59.993
A device label in
extension attribute twelve.

107
00:06:59.993 --> 00:07:04.568
And a custom security attribute that
tags each application's environment.

108
00:07:04.568 --> 00:07:06.592
Four conventions apply everywhere.

109
00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:09.692
The emergency access
exclusion is implicit.

110
00:07:09.692 --> 00:07:14.023
Every policy needs Entra ID
P1; risk policies need P2;

111
00:07:14.023 --> 00:07:18.555
workload identity policies
need Workload ID Premium.

112
00:07:18.555 --> 00:07:21.243
Every policy is created in report-only.

113
00:07:21.243 --> 00:07:25.004
And none uses the approved
client app grant,

114
00:07:25.004 --> 00:07:28.610
which became read-only on June 30, 2026.

115
00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:34.373
Policies target populations, never
people, and the populations are personas.

116
00:07:34.373 --> 00:07:37.137
They're defined in the
Federal Modernization

117
00:07:37.137 --> 00:07:39.684
Workbook's Book 02 and
kept identical here.

118
00:07:39.684 --> 00:07:43.378
Admins are users eligible for
or holding privileged roles,

119
00:07:43.378 --> 00:07:47.373
from the account-type attribute
or the PIM-eligible population.

120
00:07:47.373 --> 00:07:52.975
They carry the highest risk and get the
strictest policies: phishing-resistant MFA,

121
00:07:52.975 --> 00:07:57.522
a compliant device, trusted networks,
and privileged workstations.

122
00:07:57.522 --> 00:08:01.281
Internals are employees and
contractors with standard access,

123
00:08:01.281 --> 00:08:04.259
built dynamically from
the worker-type attribute.

124
00:08:04.259 --> 00:08:08.143
Externals are B2B guests
from partner organizations;

125
00:08:08.143 --> 00:08:11.773
Guests are ad hoc external
users from anywhere else.

126
00:08:11.773 --> 00:08:15.147
Workload identities are
single-tenant service principals.

127
00:08:15.147 --> 00:08:18.065
They must be assigned
to a policy directly,

128
00:08:18.065 --> 00:08:22.983
because a policy assigned to a group
that contains a service principal

129
00:08:22.983 --> 00:08:27.735
isn't enforced for it, and only two
policies reach them, CA015 and CA037.

130
00:08:27.735 --> 00:08:33.668
And break-glass: the two emergency accounts,
excluded from every enforced policy,

131
00:08:33.668 --> 00:08:37.210
and still recorded by CA090,
which is report-only.

132
00:08:37.210 --> 00:08:41.051
The library adds named
sub-populations inside the personas:

133
00:08:41.051 --> 00:08:45.583
web-only users without a managed
device, shared-device frontline staff,

134
00:08:45.583 --> 00:08:49.885
server administrators, Azure
operators and production operators.

135
00:08:49.885 --> 00:08:51.310
Each is a governed group.

136
00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:56.210
Now the failure mode that drives one of the
library's most important design choices.

137
00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:59.598
Persona groups are dynamic,
built from attributes.

138
00:08:59.598 --> 00:09:03.572
A user whose attribute is empty
falls outside every persona group,

139
00:09:03.572 --> 00:09:07.547
and therefore outside every policy
that includes only that group.

140
00:09:07.547 --> 00:09:09.822
That's an exclusion nobody approved.

141
00:09:09.822 --> 00:09:15.844
So the library includes all users and
excludes narrowly, and it adds CA018,

142
00:09:15.844 --> 00:09:20.779
deliberately redundant: MFA for
all users and all resources,

143
00:09:20.779 --> 00:09:23.148
with no resource exclusions.

144
00:09:23.148 --> 00:09:27.162
It uses the plain require-MFA
control rather than a strength,

145
00:09:27.162 --> 00:09:32.250
so guests who sign in with email
one-time passcodes can still satisfy it.

146
00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:37.787
The library is enforced in five
waves, each closing a class of risk.

147
00:09:37.787 --> 00:09:39.659
Wave one is the foundation,

148
00:09:39.659 --> 00:09:44.251
and it has no visible effect for
anyone already on modern clients.

149
00:09:44.251 --> 00:09:51.463
CA001 blocks legacy authentication, which
can't do MFA or present device state.

150
00:09:51.463 --> 00:09:56.334
Run the legacy query for at least two weeks
first and move every remaining client,

151
00:09:56.334 --> 00:10:00.624
including service accounts and
multifunction devices that send mail.

152
00:10:00.624 --> 00:10:04.995
CA020 requires a managed device
to register security information,

153
00:10:04.995 --> 00:10:08.975
so an attacker with a password
can't register their own method.

154
00:10:08.975 --> 00:10:13.380
CA021 blocks device code flow
and authentication transfer,

155
00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:16.200
the flows used in device-code phishing.

156
00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:20.037
And CA090, the probe, never moves to On.

157
00:10:20.037 --> 00:10:22.187
Wave two is administrators.

158
00:10:22.187 --> 00:10:27.066
CA003 requires the separate-device strength
with a four-hour sign-in frequency,

159
00:10:27.066 --> 00:10:31.427
targeting the Admins persona and the
highly privileged built-in roles,

160
00:10:31.427 --> 00:10:36.675
because role targeting also catches an
assignment made outside the persona process.

161
00:10:36.675 --> 00:10:43.902
CA004 requires a compliant device, CA005
blocks untrusted locations, and CA025

162
00:10:43.902 --> 00:10:50.800
blocks the admin portals and Azure
management from anything that isn't a PAW.

163
00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:54.891
That block is written as an
exclusion of devices labeled PAW:

164
00:10:54.891 --> 00:10:59.392
a positive-operator filter never
matches an unregistered device,

165
00:10:59.392 --> 00:11:03.238
so an unregistered device isn't
excluded, and it's blocked.

166
00:11:03.238 --> 00:11:09.143
CA026 gives server and Arc administration
the same assurance, and CA027 blocks

167
00:11:09.143 --> 00:11:15.049
the Azure management plane for everyone
who isn't an administrator or operator.

168
00:11:15.049 --> 00:11:21.439
Wave three is the one users notice: the
workforce MFA baseline, managed devices,

169
00:11:21.439 --> 00:11:27.828
the country block, partner and guest
policies, mobile app protection, the CA018

170
00:11:27.828 --> 00:11:29.401
catch-all, and CA038,

171
00:11:29.401 --> 00:11:34.512
without which CA011's limited
guest access isn't limited at all.

172
00:11:34.512 --> 00:11:36.874
Wave four is risk and workloads.

173
00:11:36.874 --> 00:11:40.486
Wave five is segments and the end state:

174
00:11:40.486 --> 00:11:45.250
CA022, phishing-resistant
MFA for every member.

175
00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:47.863
Why does the library look this way?

176
00:11:47.863 --> 00:11:53.912
Much of it is the answer to CISA's BOD
25-01, which requires federal civilian

177
00:11:53.912 --> 00:11:59.713
agencies to implement CISA's mandatory
configurations for in-scope cloud tenants.

178
00:11:59.713 --> 00:12:05.124
The deadline for the configurations
effective at issuance was June 20, 2025.

179
00:12:05.124 --> 00:12:09.375
The table lists the Entra ID items
that Conditional Access answers.

180
00:12:09.375 --> 00:12:13.325
Legacy authentication blocked: CA001.

181
00:12:13.325 --> 00:12:16.850
High-risk users blocked: CA013.

182
00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:20.388
High-risk sign-ins blocked: CA019.

183
00:12:20.388 --> 00:12:28.938
Phishing-resistant MFA for all users:
CA022, with CA003 for administrators.

184
00:12:28.938 --> 00:12:37.925
Another MFA method until then: CA006,
CA009 and CA018, as the interim.

185
00:12:37.925 --> 00:12:43.875
And phishing-resistant MFA for
highly privileged roles: CA003.

186
00:12:43.875 --> 00:12:49.875
Two SHOULD items from the SCuBA
baseline are met by CA007 and CA020.

187
00:12:49.875 --> 00:12:54.095
The other mandatory Entra items, such
as authentication methods settings,

188
00:12:54.095 --> 00:12:57.021
application consent and
the privileged-role rules,

189
00:12:57.021 --> 00:13:01.037
aren't Conditional Access settings,
and the book covers them elsewhere.

190
00:13:01.037 --> 00:13:03.288
One conflict is worth understanding.

191
00:13:03.288 --> 00:13:07.419
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 02 adopted Microsoft's

192
00:13:07.419 --> 00:13:11.850
current recommendation for high-risk
users, require risk remediation.

193
00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:16.363
BOD 25-01 says high-risk
users shall be blocked.

194
00:13:16.363 --> 00:13:20.136
Both can't win, because when
several risk policies apply,

195
00:13:20.136 --> 00:13:22.187
block overrides remediation.

196
00:13:22.187 --> 00:13:26.701
So in any tenant within the directive's
scope, CA013's grant is Block,

197
00:13:26.701 --> 00:13:29.902
it includes all users
rather than only members,

198
00:13:29.902 --> 00:13:35.319
and recovery runs through the help desk:
an administrator confirms the account,

199
00:13:35.319 --> 00:13:38.274
resets credentials and dismisses the risk.

200
00:13:38.274 --> 00:13:43.812
Where the directive doesn't apply,
CA013 uses remediation for members.

201
00:13:43.812 --> 00:13:46.387
Last, Microsoft-managed policies.

202
00:13:46.387 --> 00:13:50.875
Microsoft creates some policies itself,
in report-only, and turns them on

203
00:13:50.875 --> 00:13:55.288
no less than thirty days later unless
an administrator sets them to Off.

204
00:13:55.288 --> 00:13:59.491
The program writes down a decision
for each, adopt or supersede,

205
00:13:59.491 --> 00:14:04.988
because a policy Microsoft enables on a
schedule is a change the agency didn't make.

206
00:14:04.988 --> 00:14:08.160
Five are superseded by library policies.

207
00:14:08.910 --> 00:14:12.598
Module two: getting the
library into production safely.

208
00:14:12.598 --> 00:14:15.383
A policy that targets
all users can lock out

209
00:14:15.383 --> 00:14:18.634
the whole organization,
administrators included.

210
00:14:18.634 --> 00:14:21.841
So this module is about the
gates every policy passes,

211
00:14:21.841 --> 00:14:26.213
how to read report-only results and
roll back without deleting anything,

212
00:14:26.213 --> 00:14:30.658
why every exclusion is a governed
object, how the break-glass accounts are

213
00:14:30.658 --> 00:14:35.610
built and watched, and what the sign-in
logs must show once the policies are on.

214
00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:40.273
Every policy, in every
wave, passes the same gates.

215
00:14:40.273 --> 00:14:44.424
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 02 set the durations,

216
00:14:44.424 --> 00:14:49.047
and Microsoft's own minimum is a week
in report-only before enforcement.

217
00:14:49.047 --> 00:14:51.861
Gate one is report-only,
two to four weeks,

218
00:14:51.861 --> 00:14:55.186
while impact, gaps and
exclusions are reviewed.

219
00:14:55.186 --> 00:14:59.847
Gate two is a pilot ring of IT
and security staff for two weeks.

220
00:14:59.847 --> 00:15:03.754
Gate three expands ring by
ring, one to two weeks each:

221
00:15:03.754 --> 00:15:08.660
headquarters, then field and remote
staff, then everyone in scope.

222
00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:12.897
Then steady state: enforced,
monitored, with drift alerts.

223
00:15:12.897 --> 00:15:16.722
Inside that, the chapter
consolidates a seven-step cycle.

224
00:15:16.722 --> 00:15:21.662
One, a pre-deployment check: prerequisites
exist, readiness gates are met, the

225
00:15:21.662 --> 00:15:23.779
emergency exclusion is present,

226
00:15:23.779 --> 00:15:27.385
and the policy JSON has been
reviewed in source control.

227
00:15:27.385 --> 00:15:30.222
Two, create the policy in report-only.

228
00:15:30.222 --> 00:15:35.770
One detail: a report-only policy that
requires a compliant device can prompt users

229
00:15:35.770 --> 00:15:38.870
on macOS, iOS and Android
to pick a certificate,

230
00:15:38.870 --> 00:15:42.623
so leave those platforms out
of the report-only version.

231
00:15:42.623 --> 00:15:45.285
Three, measure for at least seven days.

232
00:15:45.285 --> 00:15:49.125
Four, analyze and remediate:
missing registrations,

233
00:15:49.125 --> 00:15:53.947
noncompliant devices, untagged
applications, empty attributes.

234
00:15:53.947 --> 00:15:58.198
Fix the cause; add an exclusion
only as a governed object.

235
00:15:58.198 --> 00:16:02.284
Five, security and business owners
approve the impact analysis,

236
00:16:02.284 --> 00:16:05.136
and that approval is
kept as change evidence.

237
00:16:05.136 --> 00:16:08.435
Six, enforce for the
pilot ring and expand.

238
00:16:08.435 --> 00:16:13.735
Turning a policy that targets all users
straight from off to on is never allowed.

239
00:16:13.735 --> 00:16:17.010
Seven, watch for forty-eight
hours after each ring.

240
00:16:17.010 --> 00:16:19.726
The readiness gates are
the program's numbers:

241
00:16:19.726 --> 00:16:24.373
at least ninety-five percent of in-scope
users have a method that satisfies the

242
00:16:24.373 --> 00:16:27.732
policy's strength, and at
least ninety percent of devices

243
00:16:27.732 --> 00:16:31.235
are enrolled and compliant,
with the attributes populated.

244
00:16:31.235 --> 00:16:35.385
The point is that each wave starts with
a measured number, not an estimate.

245
00:16:35.385 --> 00:16:37.836
And a wave starts only
after the previous one

246
00:16:37.836 --> 00:16:40.830
has been enforced for a
week without an incident.

247
00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:45.305
Report-only is a policy state,
not a test environment.

248
00:16:45.305 --> 00:16:49.870
The policy is evaluated at every
sign-in and its result is logged,

249
00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:51.592
but nothing is enforced.

250
00:16:51.592 --> 00:16:54.892
There are four results, and you'll
see them in the sign-in logs

251
00:16:54.892 --> 00:16:58.192
and in Microsoft Graph under
the names in the middle column.

252
00:16:58.192 --> 00:17:03.361
Success means the conditions matched
and the controls were already satisfied,

253
00:17:03.361 --> 00:17:06.493
for example an MFA claim
was already in the token.

254
00:17:06.493 --> 00:17:10.036
Failure means the conditions
matched and a non-interactive

255
00:17:10.036 --> 00:17:12.517
control failed: that's
what a block would do.

256
00:17:12.517 --> 00:17:16.436
User action required, which Graph
calls report-only interrupted,

257
00:17:16.436 --> 00:17:18.830
means the user would have been prompted.

258
00:17:18.830 --> 00:17:21.913
And not applied means the
conditions didn't match,

259
00:17:21.913 --> 00:17:24.093
often because the user is excluded.

260
00:17:24.093 --> 00:17:28.518
Two limits: user-action policies
can't run in report-only at all,

261
00:17:28.518 --> 00:17:33.030
and GPS-based named locations
still prompt users for location.

262
00:17:33.030 --> 00:17:37.849
To change an enforced policy, Microsoft's
practice is to create a report-only

263
00:17:37.849 --> 00:17:41.980
copy with the change, compare the
two, and then update the original.

264
00:17:41.980 --> 00:17:45.680
Rollback has four forms,
from smallest to largest.

265
00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:50.105
Revert one policy to report-only when
you need data while you investigate.

266
00:17:50.105 --> 00:17:55.041
Reduce scope with a time-bound exclusion
group when one population is blocked,

267
00:17:55.041 --> 00:17:57.243
fix the cause, then remove the group.

268
00:17:57.243 --> 00:18:00.643
Turn a policy Off when it's
causing widespread blocks.

269
00:18:00.643 --> 00:18:04.036
And when several policies
in a wave interact badly,

270
00:18:04.036 --> 00:18:07.592
set the whole wave to
report-only and open an incident.

271
00:18:07.592 --> 00:18:09.880
What's never on the list is deletion.

272
00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:14.815
Microsoft does let you restore a deleted
policy or named location within thirty

273
00:18:14.815 --> 00:18:19.234
days, but a deletion breaks the policy's
change history in source control,

274
00:18:19.234 --> 00:18:21.517
and the drift record has to explain it.

275
00:18:21.517 --> 00:18:23.847
If the tenant is locked out entirely,

276
00:18:23.847 --> 00:18:27.342
an emergency account signs
in and reverses the change.

277
00:18:27.342 --> 00:18:28.855
If no administrator can,

278
00:18:28.855 --> 00:18:33.670
Microsoft support can update the
blocking policy after verification.

279
00:18:34.420 --> 00:18:39.717
Because an exclusion overrides everything,
each one is a standing hole in policy,

280
00:18:39.717 --> 00:18:43.908
and exclusions accumulate the
way Group Policy exceptions did.

281
00:18:43.908 --> 00:18:47.744
So the program treats every
exclusion as a governed object.

282
00:18:47.744 --> 00:18:52.577
The only exclusion that's permanent by
design is the emergency access group,

283
00:18:52.577 --> 00:18:55.032
which every enforced policy excludes.

284
00:18:55.032 --> 00:18:59.445
The others in the library are each
tied to one policy and one reason.

285
00:18:59.445 --> 00:19:03.674
Approved travel lets a traveler
past CA008's country block,

286
00:19:03.674 --> 00:19:07.817
granted through PIM for Groups
after an approved request,

287
00:19:07.817 --> 00:19:13.082
for at most fourteen days, and the
traveler must still meet CA006 and CA018.

288
00:19:13.082 --> 00:19:17.474
The device code flow group holds
Teams Rooms and similar devices that

289
00:19:17.474 --> 00:19:22.094
genuinely need that flow, ideally
narrowed further in a companion policy.

290
00:19:22.094 --> 00:19:27.148
The onboarding group lets remote new
hires who don't have a managed device yet

291
00:19:27.148 --> 00:19:28.884
register MFA for a few days,

292
00:19:28.884 --> 00:19:32.595
using a Temporary Access Pass
from the pre-hire workflow.

293
00:19:32.595 --> 00:19:36.946
And the not-ready group for
CA022 is the enrollment backlog:

294
00:19:36.946 --> 00:19:41.120
it shrinks as users enroll,
and every member has an expiry.

295
00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:43.408
The rules on the right
apply to all of them.

296
00:19:43.408 --> 00:19:47.469
An exclusion is a group, never
a user named in a policy.

297
00:19:47.469 --> 00:19:52.820
It has an owner, a reason, an expiry, and
a place in the access review schedule.

298
00:19:52.820 --> 00:19:58.326
Where a script or tool signs in as a user,
the fix is a managed identity or workload

299
00:19:58.326 --> 00:20:04.082
identity, not an exclusion; the directory
sync account is the documented exception.

300
00:20:04.082 --> 00:20:06.806
And test the exclusion,
not just the policy,

301
00:20:06.806 --> 00:20:11.008
because an excluded user can still
be prompted by another policy.

302
00:20:11.008 --> 00:20:13.033
The opposite failure is quieter.

303
00:20:13.033 --> 00:20:18.620
A policy that includes a dynamic group
misses every user whose attribute is empty.

304
00:20:18.620 --> 00:20:20.803
That's an exclusion nobody approved,

305
00:20:20.803 --> 00:20:25.751
which is why the baseline policies include
all users and exclude narrowly, and why

306
00:20:25.751 --> 00:20:31.210
attribute completeness is checked before
any attribute-scoped policy is enforced.

307
00:20:31.960 --> 00:20:37.211
Every control that blocks an administrative
path also creates a way to lock the

308
00:20:37.211 --> 00:20:40.154
agency out of its own
tenant: federation down,

309
00:20:40.154 --> 00:20:44.848
every administrator's MFA device
unavailable, a misconfigured policy,

310
00:20:44.848 --> 00:20:48.985
or every privileged role eligible
with no approver able to act.

311
00:20:48.985 --> 00:20:52.338
Emergency access accounts,
break-glass, are the answer,

312
00:20:52.338 --> 00:20:56.660
and they're the only standing privilege
the program accepts by design.

313
00:20:56.660 --> 00:20:58.410
The standard is on the left.

314
00:20:58.410 --> 00:21:03.503
Two or more cloud-only accounts on the
tenant's onmicrosoft dot com domain, neither

315
00:21:03.503 --> 00:21:08.222
synchronized nor federated, and not tied
to one person or an employee's phone.

316
00:21:08.222 --> 00:21:13.056
Permanent active Global Administrator;
together with service principals that can't

317
00:21:13.056 --> 00:21:17.685
perform an activation, they're the only
standing active assignments in the tenant.

318
00:21:17.685 --> 00:21:22.040
Phishing-resistant authentication,
a FIDO2 passkey or certificate-based

319
00:21:22.040 --> 00:21:26.735
authentication, using methods different
from the normal administrator accounts.

320
00:21:26.735 --> 00:21:31.104
The older pattern of a password in
sealed envelopes is superseded,

321
00:21:31.104 --> 00:21:34.281
because a password alone
can no longer reach the

322
00:21:34.281 --> 00:21:37.935
admin portals where Microsoft's
mandatory MFA applies.

323
00:21:37.935 --> 00:21:41.913
Excluded from every policy that
blocks or restricts sign-in,

324
00:21:41.913 --> 00:21:43.823
through one dedicated group.

325
00:21:43.823 --> 00:21:46.896
Validated at least every
ninety days by signing

326
00:21:46.896 --> 00:21:49.585
in and performing an administrative task.

327
00:21:49.585 --> 00:21:51.735
On the right is how
the build watches them.

328
00:21:51.735 --> 00:21:55.164
Report-only policies
don't need the exclusion,

329
00:21:55.164 --> 00:21:59.135
so CA090 is the one policy
that records their sign-ins.

330
00:21:59.135 --> 00:22:01.488
Alerts match the accounts' object IDs,

331
00:22:01.488 --> 00:22:05.360
because their names deliberately
don't reveal their purpose.

332
00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:10.048
Every use is a critical incident
until a post-use review closes it.

333
00:22:10.048 --> 00:22:15.595
And because NIST's AC-2(2) expects emergency
accounts to be disabled automatically

334
00:22:15.595 --> 00:22:20.746
after a defined period, the system security
plan records these as a documented

335
00:22:20.746 --> 00:22:22.568
deviation, with monitoring,

336
00:22:22.568 --> 00:22:27.323
post-use review and the ninety-day
validation as compensating controls.

337
00:22:27.323 --> 00:22:28.709
Finally, order.

338
00:22:28.709 --> 00:22:32.285
The accounts exist and pass
a test before any role

339
00:22:32.285 --> 00:22:35.685
is made eligible or any
approval is switched on.

340
00:22:35.685 --> 00:22:39.290
Module three explains the
lockout that prevents.

341
00:22:40.040 --> 00:22:43.565
Once policies are on, the
sign-in logs are the instrument.

342
00:22:43.565 --> 00:22:46.828
The queries run against the
SigninLogs table in a Log

343
00:22:46.828 --> 00:22:50.165
Analytics workspace that
receives Entra sign-in logs.

344
00:22:50.165 --> 00:22:51.828
The chapter gives them in full;

345
00:22:51.828 --> 00:22:56.220
they're illustrative and haven't been
tested against a production workspace, so

346
00:22:56.220 --> 00:22:58.682
confirm column values
against your own data

347
00:22:58.682 --> 00:23:01.077
before using them for
alerting or evidence.

348
00:23:01.077 --> 00:23:02.552
Six signals matter.

349
00:23:02.552 --> 00:23:07.577
Per-policy results over seven days, including
report-only outcomes; after wave two,

350
00:23:07.577 --> 00:23:10.163
members of the Admins group should show no

351
00:23:10.163 --> 00:23:13.415
not-applied results for
the administrator policies.

352
00:23:13.415 --> 00:23:18.218
Blocked sign-ins, error 53003, which
is blocked by Conditional Access;

353
00:23:18.218 --> 00:23:22.010
a spike on a single application
usually means a client

354
00:23:22.010 --> 00:23:25.802
that can't do modern
authentication or app protection.

355
00:23:25.802 --> 00:23:31.252
The end-state gap: users who would
fail CA022 while it's in report-only.

356
00:23:31.252 --> 00:23:34.128
That's the list that drives
the enrollment campaign.

357
00:23:34.128 --> 00:23:38.430
Legacy authentication still
in use, over fourteen days,

358
00:23:38.430 --> 00:23:41.891
which must be empty
before CA001 is enforced.

359
00:23:41.891 --> 00:23:46.179
Coverage gaps: successful sign-ins
that no policy applied to,

360
00:23:46.179 --> 00:23:50.640
which should be empty for interactive
sign-ins once CA018 is on.

361
00:23:50.640 --> 00:23:53.869
And emergency account use,
matched on object IDs,

362
00:23:53.869 --> 00:23:57.340
which should be zero outside
scheduled validation.

363
00:23:57.340 --> 00:23:58.303
Then drift.

364
00:23:58.303 --> 00:24:02.759
The policy set in the tenant must
equal the policy set in source control,

365
00:24:02.759 --> 00:24:07.670
and the drift check alerts on a policy
whose state changed without an approved

366
00:24:07.670 --> 00:24:10.919
change; a change to any
exclusion group's membership,

367
00:24:10.919 --> 00:24:15.905
especially the emergency access group;
a change to conditions, named locations,

368
00:24:15.905 --> 00:24:18.398
authentication strengths or controls;

369
00:24:18.398 --> 00:24:21.495
a policy created or deleted
outside the pipeline,

370
00:24:21.495 --> 00:24:25.953
including a Microsoft-managed policy
that Microsoft added or enabled;

371
00:24:25.953 --> 00:24:30.302
and users or devices missing the
attributes that put them in scope.

372
00:24:30.302 --> 00:24:34.883
Protected actions, which require
fresh strong authentication to change

373
00:24:34.883 --> 00:24:39.540
policies and named locations, narrow
who can cause drift in the first place.

374
00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:41.610
Module three builds them.

375
00:24:42.360 --> 00:24:47.160
Module three: Privileged Identity
Management, self-request with approval.

376
00:24:47.160 --> 00:24:51.460
An eligible role is only as safe
as the moment it turns active.

377
00:24:51.460 --> 00:24:54.348
So that moment needs
fresh proof, a reason,

378
00:24:54.348 --> 00:24:58.510
a second person, and a record
that outlives the audit window.

379
00:24:58.510 --> 00:25:03.066
We'll cover the two layers of self-request,
the activation controls by tier,

380
00:25:03.066 --> 00:25:06.501
the build in the order that
avoids locking yourself out,

381
00:25:06.501 --> 00:25:10.310
and the approvers, alerts and
records an assessor will ask for.

382
00:25:10.310 --> 00:25:13.160
Appendix B has every role's settings.

383
00:25:13.910 --> 00:25:18.460
Self-request has two layers, and
an agency can run either or both.

384
00:25:18.460 --> 00:25:23.535
Self-request for eligibility is a person
asking to become eligible for a role.

385
00:25:23.535 --> 00:25:27.883
It's decided in entitlement management,
through an access package for the

386
00:25:27.883 --> 00:25:32.160
role-assignable group, and it answers:
may this person ever hold this role?

387
00:25:32.160 --> 00:25:35.478
Self-request for activation
is an already-eligible

388
00:25:35.478 --> 00:25:38.873
person asking to turn the
role on for a bounded window.

389
00:25:38.873 --> 00:25:43.697
It's decided in PIM, and it answers:
may this person use this role right now?

390
00:25:43.697 --> 00:25:46.557
Microsoft's guidance
for the DoD Zero Trust

391
00:25:46.557 --> 00:25:49.259
user pillar treats the two as composable.

392
00:25:49.259 --> 00:25:55.359
This module builds the second layer,
because it produces a per-use, time-bounded,

393
00:25:55.359 --> 00:25:59.300
individually justified
record of privileged use,

394
00:25:59.300 --> 00:26:02.960
which is what AC-6(9) and
AC-17(4) are tested against.

395
00:26:02.960 --> 00:26:05.480
Eligibility without an activation gate is

396
00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:08.210
standing privilege through
a nicer front door.

397
00:26:08.210 --> 00:26:12.098
If an agency runs only one
layer, it should run this one.

398
00:26:12.098 --> 00:26:14.035
The sequence is six steps.

399
00:26:14.035 --> 00:26:17.868
The administrator requests
activation with a duration within

400
00:26:17.868 --> 00:26:21.123
the role's maximum, a
justification and a ticket number.

401
00:26:21.123 --> 00:26:24.419
PIM sends them through
authentication context c1,

402
00:26:24.419 --> 00:26:27.636
which demands a fresh
phishing-resistant sign-in.

403
00:26:27.636 --> 00:26:30.747
The request goes pending,
and approvers are emailed.

404
00:26:30.747 --> 00:26:34.798
An approver from outside the
requester's tier approves or denies,

405
00:26:34.798 --> 00:26:36.673
with their own justification.

406
00:26:36.673 --> 00:26:40.042
On approval, the role is
active until the requested

407
00:26:40.042 --> 00:26:43.172
end time and returns to
eligible with no action.

408
00:26:43.172 --> 00:26:46.216
Denied, or undecided
after twenty-four hours,

409
00:26:46.216 --> 00:26:49.672
the role stays eligible and
a new request is needed.

410
00:26:49.672 --> 00:26:51.460
One dependency people miss.

411
00:26:51.460 --> 00:26:56.510
If the P2 or Governance license lapses,
permanent assignments are unaffected,

412
00:26:56.510 --> 00:26:59.696
but eligible Entra role
assignments are removed,

413
00:26:59.696 --> 00:27:02.648
PIM settings are removed
and role reviews end.

414
00:27:02.648 --> 00:27:06.426
Treat a license expiry as a
configuration-management event,

415
00:27:06.426 --> 00:27:11.090
and keep the role inventory exported
so eligibility can be restored.

416
00:27:11.840 --> 00:27:13.690
PIM settings are defined per role.

417
00:27:13.690 --> 00:27:16.976
Each role's settings are
independent of every other role's,

418
00:27:16.976 --> 00:27:19.440
and every assignment of
a role follows them.

419
00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:23.703
The program sets them by tier,
from Chapter 04's tier model.

420
00:27:23.703 --> 00:27:27.146
Tier 0 is the control plane:
Global, Privileged Role,

421
00:27:27.146 --> 00:27:32.477
Privileged Authentication, Security
and Conditional Access Administrator.

422
00:27:32.477 --> 00:27:38.293
Four-hour activations, authentication context
c1, justification and ticket required,

423
00:27:38.293 --> 00:27:40.797
approval by the Tier 0 approver group,

424
00:27:40.797 --> 00:27:44.028
and eligibility that
expires after ninety days.

425
00:27:44.028 --> 00:27:47.662
Tier 1, the management plane,
such as Exchange, SharePoint,

426
00:27:47.662 --> 00:27:51.297
Intune and Application
Administrator, gets eight hours,

427
00:27:51.297 --> 00:27:54.777
the Tier 1 approver group,
and a hundred and eighty days.

428
00:27:54.777 --> 00:27:59.540
Tier 2, scoped operations inside
administrative units, gets eight hours,

429
00:27:59.540 --> 00:28:04.064
and approval is optional, with a
documented risk acceptance per role.

430
00:28:04.064 --> 00:28:06.268
Read-only roles need no approval,

431
00:28:06.268 --> 00:28:11.165
but still pass c1 with a justification,
and their eligibility runs a year.

432
00:28:11.165 --> 00:28:15.565
Now, what the controls really do,
because the old guides got several wrong.

433
00:28:15.565 --> 00:28:19.208
The activation maximum runs
from one to twenty-four hours,

434
00:28:19.208 --> 00:28:23.078
and it's the exposure window, so
set the shortest the work needs.

435
00:28:23.078 --> 00:28:27.835
Require MFA on activation may not
prompt at all if the user authenticated

436
00:28:27.835 --> 00:28:32.515
strongly earlier in the session, so
it proves nothing at activation time.

437
00:28:32.515 --> 00:28:36.706
That's why every role uses the
authentication context instead, which is

438
00:28:36.706 --> 00:28:41.465
Microsoft's recommendation when the goal
is authentication during activation.

439
00:28:41.465 --> 00:28:46.332
Ticket information is recorded but never
checked against a ticketing system;

440
00:28:46.332 --> 00:28:49.327
enforced correlation
belongs in the SIEM or ITSM.

441
00:28:49.327 --> 00:28:53.090
And approval needs at least one
approver, with two recommended.

442
00:28:53.090 --> 00:28:57.162
The assignment controls close the
side door, so a well-built gate can't

443
00:28:57.162 --> 00:29:01.377
be bypassed by simply granting
someone a permanent active assignment.

444
00:29:01.377 --> 00:29:03.728
No permanent eligible assignments.

445
00:29:03.728 --> 00:29:08.840
No permanent active ones, except Global
Administrator for the emergency accounts.

446
00:29:08.840 --> 00:29:13.439
And MFA and justification are required
when an active assignment is made,

447
00:29:13.439 --> 00:29:18.930
because PIM can't apply an activation
requirement to someone who's already active.

448
00:29:19.680 --> 00:29:21.043
Work the build in order.

449
00:29:21.043 --> 00:29:23.715
Step zero isn't optional
and can't be moved:

450
00:29:23.715 --> 00:29:28.038
configuring approval before
emergency access accounts exist is

451
00:29:28.038 --> 00:29:31.968
the one sequence that can lock
an agency out of its own tenant.

452
00:29:31.968 --> 00:29:33.605
Microsoft documents it.

453
00:29:33.605 --> 00:29:38.372
Every Privileged Role and Global
Administrator assignment is eligible,

454
00:29:38.372 --> 00:29:41.888
approval is required, and no
approvers are configured,

455
00:29:41.888 --> 00:29:46.030
so the default approvers, the
active administrators, don't exist.

456
00:29:46.030 --> 00:29:50.487
Avoid it with both halves of the fix:
emergency accounts with permanent active

457
00:29:50.487 --> 00:29:55.218
Global Administrator, and named approver
groups on every role that needs approval.

458
00:29:55.218 --> 00:29:58.655
Step one builds those approver
groups, one per tier.

459
00:29:58.655 --> 00:30:03.736
Step two builds authentication context
c1 and its Conditional Access policy:

460
00:30:03.736 --> 00:30:09.051
the separate-device strength and a compliant
device, sign-in frequency every time,

461
00:30:09.051 --> 00:30:13.818
excluding the emergency group, and
turned On, because Microsoft requires

462
00:30:13.818 --> 00:30:18.430
the policy to exist and be enabled
before the context is selected in PIM.

463
00:30:18.430 --> 00:30:19.730
Three constraints.

464
00:30:19.730 --> 00:30:22.323
Don't scope that policy
to a directory role,

465
00:30:22.323 --> 00:30:25.730
because at activation the user
doesn't hold the role yet.

466
00:30:25.730 --> 00:30:31.137
The fallback to plain MFA happens only
when no policy targets the context;

467
00:30:31.137 --> 00:30:36.455
it doesn't trigger if the policy is off,
in report-only or excludes the user.

468
00:30:36.455 --> 00:30:41.933
And the context governs
activation, not use, so CA003,

469
00:30:41.933 --> 00:30:47.793
CA004 and CA025 still apply
to every privileged sign-in.

470
00:30:47.793 --> 00:30:50.496
Steps three and four apply role settings,

471
00:30:50.496 --> 00:30:54.392
in the portal for one role and
in Microsoft Graph for thirty.

472
00:30:54.392 --> 00:30:56.818
Each role's policy has seventeen rules.

473
00:30:56.818 --> 00:31:01.886
Step five converts standing assignments:
create the eligible assignment first,

474
00:31:01.886 --> 00:31:05.593
confirm the person can activate,
then remove the active one.

475
00:31:05.593 --> 00:31:08.268
Step six closes a gap PIM leaves.

476
00:31:08.268 --> 00:31:11.661
PIM protects becoming a
Conditional Access Administrator,

477
00:31:11.661 --> 00:31:15.055
but not editing a policy once
someone holds that permission.

478
00:31:15.055 --> 00:31:19.955
Protected actions attach a second
context, c2, to those permissions.

479
00:31:19.955 --> 00:31:23.591
Steps seven to nine are
notifications and alerts,

480
00:31:23.591 --> 00:31:26.870
reviews, and log export,
on the next slide.

481
00:31:27.620 --> 00:31:31.008
The approval engine has three
properties that shape the design.

482
00:31:31.008 --> 00:31:33.421
Approvers don't need to hold any role.

483
00:31:33.421 --> 00:31:38.061
Approvers can't approve their own requests,
and service principals can't approve

484
00:31:38.061 --> 00:31:42.633
at all; that's enforced by the platform,
which is worth saying in the security plan.

485
00:31:42.633 --> 00:31:45.969
And approvers need a P2
or Governance license.

486
00:31:45.969 --> 00:31:48.345
From those, the separation rules.

487
00:31:48.345 --> 00:31:52.620
A tier's approvers aren't members
of that tier's eligible population;

488
00:31:52.620 --> 00:31:56.437
otherwise the approvers and the
requesters are the same set,

489
00:31:56.437 --> 00:31:59.033
and approval degrades to mutual sign-off.

490
00:31:59.033 --> 00:32:03.432
The platform blocks self-approval;
only group design blocks collusion.

491
00:32:03.432 --> 00:32:05.496
At least two approvers per tier,

492
00:32:05.496 --> 00:32:09.307
held as governed groups,
because named individuals leave.

493
00:32:09.307 --> 00:32:12.989
Tier 0 approvers sit outside
the Tier 0 population,

494
00:32:12.989 --> 00:32:15.532
for example in the security office.

495
00:32:15.532 --> 00:32:18.554
The approver's own
justification is required,

496
00:32:18.554 --> 00:32:21.257
so the record holds a
decision, not a click.

497
00:32:21.257 --> 00:32:25.083
And approver groups are reviewed like
the privileged objects they are.

498
00:32:25.083 --> 00:32:27.670
On the right, the signals and records.

499
00:32:27.670 --> 00:32:31.395
The most important PIM alert
is roles assigned outside PIM,

500
00:32:31.395 --> 00:32:35.957
high severity, because an assignment
made outside PIM never met the gate.

501
00:32:35.957 --> 00:32:37.852
It detects rather than prevents;

502
00:32:37.852 --> 00:32:41.921
limiting who holds Privileged Role
Administrator is the prevention.

503
00:32:41.921 --> 00:32:46.096
The approval window is a fixed
twenty-four hours with a single stage,

504
00:32:46.096 --> 00:32:50.793
so a Tier 0 path staffed by two people in
one time zone will strand out-of-hours

505
00:32:50.793 --> 00:32:54.894
requests; the documented answer
to "the approvers are unavailable

506
00:32:54.894 --> 00:32:59.070
and production is down" is the
emergency procedure, not a workaround.

507
00:32:59.070 --> 00:33:03.691
Role reviews run monthly for Tier 0
and quarterly otherwise, and never as

508
00:33:03.691 --> 00:33:08.620
self-review, because self-review leaves
role-assignable groups out entirely.

509
00:33:08.620 --> 00:33:11.735
And PIM's audit history keeps thirty days,

510
00:33:11.735 --> 00:33:15.308
so the logs are exported
for AU-11 before go-live.

511
00:33:15.308 --> 00:33:20.083
One date to act on: the
legacy beta privileged-access

512
00:33:20.083 --> 00:33:24.082
API stops returning data
on October 28, 2026.

513
00:33:24.082 --> 00:33:26.740
Anything still calling it must move now.

514
00:33:27.490 --> 00:33:30.065
Module four: access governance.

515
00:33:30.065 --> 00:33:33.701
Conditional Access decides
whether a session may proceed,

516
00:33:33.701 --> 00:33:37.115
and PIM decides whether a
privileged role may be active.

517
00:33:37.115 --> 00:33:39.639
Neither decides whether
a person should still

518
00:33:39.639 --> 00:33:41.840
hold the access they were given last year.

519
00:33:41.840 --> 00:33:46.715
A grant nobody re-confirms is a
decision made once and trusted forever.

520
00:33:46.715 --> 00:33:50.850
Three mechanisms close that gap
without anyone opening a ticket:

521
00:33:50.850 --> 00:33:56.180
requests through access packages, reviews
on a calendar, and lifecycle events.

522
00:33:56.930 --> 00:34:02.012
Entitlement management automates access
requests, assignments, reviews and

523
00:34:02.012 --> 00:34:05.844
expiration for groups,
applications and SharePoint sites,

524
00:34:05.844 --> 00:34:07.955
for internal and external users.

525
00:34:07.955 --> 00:34:09.842
It has three building blocks.

526
00:34:09.842 --> 00:34:13.078
A catalog is the container
and the unit of delegation;

527
00:34:13.078 --> 00:34:17.415
the program creates one per business
domain, with the domain's business

528
00:34:17.415 --> 00:34:21.605
owner as catalog owner, and doesn't
use the automatic General catalog.

529
00:34:21.605 --> 00:34:24.417
An access package bundles resource roles.

530
00:34:24.417 --> 00:34:29.381
And a policy says who may request, who
approves, and when the assignment ends.

531
00:34:29.381 --> 00:34:34.305
The approval chains follow the application's
risk tier, as design positions.

532
00:34:34.305 --> 00:34:35.996
Tier 3: the department owner,

533
00:34:35.996 --> 00:34:41.142
which is the requestor's manager with a
fallback approver for anyone with no manager

534
00:34:41.142 --> 00:34:44.670
in the directory, seven days to
decide, and a one-year expiry

535
00:34:44.670 --> 00:34:48.493
that extends without approval
while the user is still in scope.

536
00:34:48.493 --> 00:34:53.025
Tier 2: the application owner, with
the backup owner as alternate,

537
00:34:53.025 --> 00:34:55.505
five days, a hundred and eighty days.

538
00:34:55.505 --> 00:34:57.290
Tier 1 adds a second stage,

539
00:34:57.290 --> 00:35:01.455
the identity governance office,
and expires in ninety days.

540
00:35:01.455 --> 00:35:05.435
The earlier procedure only notified
the office of Tier 1 grants;

541
00:35:05.435 --> 00:35:10.467
a second stage costs a few hours and leaves
an approval record instead of an email.

542
00:35:10.467 --> 00:35:14.918
External users get a separate,
stricter policy and at most a year.

543
00:35:14.918 --> 00:35:19.718
The program never uses "never."
Underneath is the group substrate.

544
00:35:19.718 --> 00:35:23.818
Applications are assigned to
groups, never to users directly.

545
00:35:23.818 --> 00:35:25.668
Nested groups are prohibited,

546
00:35:25.668 --> 00:35:30.627
because group assignment to an application
gives access only to direct members,

547
00:35:30.627 --> 00:35:35.068
and a group review shows nested users
but can only remove direct members.

548
00:35:35.068 --> 00:35:40.143
And names follow one convention, so a
group's purpose and tier can be read from it.

549
00:35:40.143 --> 00:35:45.166
Separation of duties: a package can
list incompatible packages and groups,

550
00:35:45.166 --> 00:35:49.055
but the check is one-way, so each
package must list the other.

551
00:35:49.055 --> 00:35:51.961
A legitimate exception
becomes its own combined

552
00:35:51.961 --> 00:35:55.292
package with a shorter expiry
and more frequent reviews.

553
00:35:55.292 --> 00:35:58.665
And privileged access arrives
through PIM eligibility,

554
00:35:58.665 --> 00:36:02.030
never through a package
that grants an active role.

555
00:36:02.780 --> 00:36:05.336
Access reviews are recurring attestation

556
00:36:05.336 --> 00:36:07.892
that each person still
needs what they hold.

557
00:36:07.892 --> 00:36:11.540
Each instance captures a snapshot
of access when it starts,

558
00:36:11.540 --> 00:36:14.742
and changes during a review
show up in the next cycle.

559
00:36:14.742 --> 00:36:16.730
The schedule is set by risk.

560
00:36:16.730 --> 00:36:21.910
Privileged roles are reviewed in PIM,
monthly for Tier 0 by the Tier 0 approver

561
00:36:21.910 --> 00:36:26.767
group, and quarterly for the other
tiers, with removal if nobody responds.

562
00:36:26.767 --> 00:36:31.530
Tier 1 application groups are reviewed
quarterly in two stages of seven days:

563
00:36:31.530 --> 00:36:34.581
the manager first, then
the application owner for

564
00:36:34.581 --> 00:36:37.706
everyone the manager approved
or marked don't know.

565
00:36:37.706 --> 00:36:40.692
That replaces the old
manual escalation step.

566
00:36:40.692 --> 00:36:44.010
Tier 2 groups are semiannual
and Tier 3 annual,

567
00:36:44.010 --> 00:36:46.892
by the application or department owner.

568
00:36:46.892 --> 00:36:49.814
Guests are reviewed
quarterly by their sponsors,

569
00:36:49.814 --> 00:36:52.593
and denied guests are
blocked and then removed.

570
00:36:52.593 --> 00:36:54.392
Four rules make reviews bite.

571
00:36:54.392 --> 00:36:57.755
First, review the groups,
not the applications.

572
00:36:57.755 --> 00:37:02.316
Microsoft notes that reviewing an application
that uses group assignment doesn't

573
00:37:02.316 --> 00:37:06.480
remove the members of those groups; they
keep their access through the group.

574
00:37:06.480 --> 00:37:10.244
Second, whenever reviewers
are managers or group owners,

575
00:37:10.244 --> 00:37:15.130
add a fallback reviewer for users with
no manager and groups with no owner.

576
00:37:15.130 --> 00:37:18.580
Third, choose the no-response
default deliberately.

577
00:37:18.580 --> 00:37:22.619
Microsoft warns that combining
remove access with auto-apply

578
00:37:22.619 --> 00:37:26.580
can revoke all access to a resource
if reviewers don't respond.

579
00:37:26.580 --> 00:37:31.467
So the program sets remove only where an
unreviewed grant is riskier than a lost

580
00:37:31.467 --> 00:37:35.318
one, privileged roles and guests,
and no change with escalation

581
00:37:35.318 --> 00:37:39.242
for business applications, where
a mass removal would stop work.

582
00:37:39.242 --> 00:37:42.063
Fourth, justification on every decision,

583
00:37:42.063 --> 00:37:45.131
and no bulk decisions
on privileged reviews.

584
00:37:45.131 --> 00:37:48.854
The service can't prevent a bulk
action, so the history is checked

585
00:37:48.854 --> 00:37:52.930
for one reviewer recording many
identical decisions within minutes.

586
00:37:52.930 --> 00:37:57.476
The records: results download as a
CSV, and review history reports stay

587
00:37:57.476 --> 00:38:03.170
downloadable for only thirty days, so each
one goes straight to the evidence store.

588
00:38:03.920 --> 00:38:06.583
The third mechanism is lifecycle events.

589
00:38:06.583 --> 00:38:10.672
The HR-driven lifecycle itself,
from attributes to joiner,

590
00:38:10.672 --> 00:38:16.382
mover and leaver design, belongs to the
Federal Modernization Workbook's Book 03.

591
00:38:16.382 --> 00:38:21.006
This chapter covers only the Lifecycle
Workflows tasks that change access,

592
00:38:21.006 --> 00:38:26.144
because those tasks are where mover and
leaver problems turn into access decisions.

593
00:38:26.144 --> 00:38:29.945
A joiner gets the onboarding
package and the department package,

594
00:38:29.945 --> 00:38:32.408
never a direct add to
an application group.

595
00:38:32.408 --> 00:38:35.483
A move is a re-derivation,
not an additional grant:

596
00:38:35.483 --> 00:38:40.634
remove the previous department's packages,
request the new department's package,

597
00:38:40.634 --> 00:38:45.246
and send everything the person still
holds outside packages to a review.

598
00:38:45.246 --> 00:38:49.698
Microsoft's built-in job profile change
template does the first two steps,

599
00:38:49.698 --> 00:38:53.129
and its remove-all-assignments
task defaults to fifteen

600
00:38:53.129 --> 00:38:56.195
days after the trigger, a
grace period for handover.

601
00:38:56.195 --> 00:38:59.245
The program keeps that
grace for standard access

602
00:38:59.245 --> 00:39:02.370
and sets it to zero for Tier
1 and privileged access.

603
00:39:02.370 --> 00:39:07.657
A leaver's workflow cancels pending
requests, removes every package assignment,

604
00:39:07.657 --> 00:39:12.233
removes the user from all groups and
teams, and revokes refresh tokens.

605
00:39:12.233 --> 00:39:15.231
And an inactivity workflow
is the backstop for

606
00:39:15.231 --> 00:39:18.769
accounts whose HR record
never produced a leaver event.

607
00:39:18.769 --> 00:39:23.745
A tenant can have fifty workflows, so
spend them on events, not on applications.

608
00:39:23.745 --> 00:39:27.187
Guests are where unreviewed
access piles up fastest,

609
00:39:27.187 --> 00:39:31.020
because nobody's HR record ends
when a partner project does.

610
00:39:31.020 --> 00:39:32.008
Four layers.

611
00:39:32.008 --> 00:39:33.691
Invite through a package,

612
00:39:33.691 --> 00:39:37.858
so every guest arrives with an
approver, a sponsor and an expiry.

613
00:39:37.858 --> 00:39:42.659
When a governed guest loses their last
assignment, they're blocked and, by default,

614
00:39:42.659 --> 00:39:47.318
removed after thirty days; ungoverned
guests stay in the tenant indefinitely,

615
00:39:47.318 --> 00:39:51.332
so the program marks existing guests
with assignments as governed.

616
00:39:51.332 --> 00:39:53.683
Sponsors review guests quarterly.

617
00:39:53.683 --> 00:39:56.467
And guest lifecycle
policies, still in preview,

618
00:39:56.467 --> 00:39:59.395
will be adopted when
they're generally available.

619
00:39:59.395 --> 00:40:04.358
All of this is licensed by Entra ID
Governance, so plan it for the whole workforce.

620
00:40:04.358 --> 00:40:07.378
Partial licensing produces
a program that governs

621
00:40:07.378 --> 00:40:10.270
some people and leaves
the rest ungoverned.

622
00:40:11.020 --> 00:40:15.470
Module five: access as code,
and the evidence it produces.

623
00:40:15.470 --> 00:40:20.007
An access policy that exists only
as portal clicks can't be diffed,

624
00:40:20.007 --> 00:40:23.032
reviewed or replayed, so
it can't be assessed.

625
00:40:23.032 --> 00:40:28.435
The invariant this module enforces: every
access decision has a reviewable source of

626
00:40:28.435 --> 00:40:33.457
truth, a pipeline applies that source, and
a monitor checks the tenant against it.

627
00:40:33.457 --> 00:40:36.218
Then the evidence is
produced by doing the work,

628
00:40:36.218 --> 00:40:38.850
not reconstructed for the assessment.

629
00:40:39.600 --> 00:40:43.936
Every access object the program
governs has a file in the repository,

630
00:40:43.936 --> 00:40:47.512
one per object, and the safety
rules travel with the files.

631
00:40:47.512 --> 00:40:52.090
Every blocking or grant policy
excludes the emergency access group,

632
00:40:52.090 --> 00:40:55.463
and new and changed policies
deploy as report-only.

633
00:40:55.463 --> 00:41:01.289
PIM settings require an authentication
context or MFA, justification, and approval

634
00:41:01.289 --> 00:41:06.700
for Tier 0, with no permanent active
assignment except the emergency accounts.

635
00:41:06.700 --> 00:41:10.287
Groups follow the naming
convention with no nested members.

636
00:41:10.287 --> 00:41:15.200
Packages have an expiry, and incompatible
packages are listed both ways.

637
00:41:15.200 --> 00:41:18.812
Review definitions have a
fallback reviewer and auto-apply.

638
00:41:18.812 --> 00:41:23.838
Leaver workflows remove all package
assignments and revoke refresh tokens.

639
00:41:23.838 --> 00:41:28.513
Four tools can hold those objects,
and every one ends in Microsoft Graph.

640
00:41:28.513 --> 00:41:31.381
Graph covers everything,
but holds no state,

641
00:41:31.381 --> 00:41:36.799
so desired state has to live in the repository
and the comparison has to be written.

642
00:41:36.799 --> 00:41:41.855
The Terraform azuread provider, at
version 3.10.0 as of October 2026,

643
00:41:41.855 --> 00:41:46.267
covers most classes but has
no access review definitions,

644
00:41:46.267 --> 00:41:52.425
no Lifecycle Workflows, and no role
management policy for Entra directory roles.

645
00:41:52.425 --> 00:41:59.550
Microsoft365DSC, an open-source community
module, covers every class in the table.

646
00:41:59.550 --> 00:42:03.895
And Microsoft Entra Tenant
Governance configuration management,

647
00:42:03.895 --> 00:42:06.534
built on generally available Graph APIs,

648
00:42:06.534 --> 00:42:11.112
monitors a baseline but doesn't cover
review definitions or workflows.

649
00:42:11.112 --> 00:42:13.800
The program's reference
choice minimizes write

650
00:42:13.800 --> 00:42:16.287
paths while keeping
drift detection native.

651
00:42:16.287 --> 00:42:22.017
Microsoft365DSC writes, because it's the
only declarative tool that covers every

652
00:42:22.017 --> 00:42:24.629
class; treat it as a community project,

653
00:42:24.629 --> 00:42:27.662
check its release notes,
and pin its version.

654
00:42:27.662 --> 00:42:31.416
A team already invested in Terraform
can use it where it covers,

655
00:42:31.416 --> 00:42:33.437
and Graph PowerShell for the rest.

656
00:42:33.437 --> 00:42:38.663
The rule that matters is one writer per
object class, recorded in the repository.

657
00:42:38.663 --> 00:42:41.962
The tenant monitor detects
drift where it covers;

658
00:42:41.962 --> 00:42:45.987
a daily read-only comparison
handles reviews and workflows.

659
00:42:45.987 --> 00:42:48.612
And Entra Exporter takes a weekly backup,

660
00:42:48.612 --> 00:42:52.520
kept separate from the
baselines, as Microsoft advises.

661
00:42:53.270 --> 00:42:56.745
The repository is the only
normal path to production.

662
00:42:56.745 --> 00:43:02.023
Six change-control rules, which together
are how the program meets CM-3 and

663
00:43:02.023 --> 00:43:07.733
CM-5 for the identity plane and FedRAMP
20x's change-management indicators.

664
00:43:07.733 --> 00:43:12.183
One, two people per change: the author
of a pull request can't approve it,

665
00:43:12.183 --> 00:43:16.790
and Tier 0 objects, such as emergency
exclusions and Tier 0 PIM settings,

666
00:43:16.790 --> 00:43:19.757
need a second approver
from the security team.

667
00:43:19.757 --> 00:43:24.876
Two, checks before merge: schema
validation, the emergency exclusion check,

668
00:43:24.876 --> 00:43:29.333
the safety rules, and a plan or dry
run attached to the pull request.

669
00:43:29.333 --> 00:43:34.197
Three, report-only first; a policy runs
at least a week against the sign-in

670
00:43:34.197 --> 00:43:39.144
logs and is enabled in a second pull
request that cites the impact review.

671
00:43:39.144 --> 00:43:42.337
Four, the pipeline signs
in as a service principal

672
00:43:42.337 --> 00:43:45.295
with workload identity
federation, no secret.

673
00:43:45.295 --> 00:43:49.558
It can change every access policy in
the tenant, so it's a Tier 0 asset,

674
00:43:49.558 --> 00:43:52.892
with two owners, quarterly
permission reviews and a

675
00:43:52.892 --> 00:43:56.845
workload identity policy
restricting where it signs in from.

676
00:43:56.845 --> 00:44:01.766
Five, emergency changes in the portal happen
under a PIM activation with a ticket,

677
00:44:01.766 --> 00:44:04.410
and are back-ported
within one business day;

678
00:44:04.410 --> 00:44:08.083
until then the drift monitor
reports them, which is the point.

679
00:44:08.083 --> 00:44:12.869
Six, rollback is a revert, so it
has the same record as the change.

680
00:44:12.869 --> 00:44:15.416
Drift detection catches
what code can't stop,

681
00:44:15.416 --> 00:44:19.407
because a Global Administrator can
still change the tenant directly.

682
00:44:19.407 --> 00:44:22.383
The tenant monitor's limits
shape how it's used.

683
00:44:22.383 --> 00:44:25.082
It runs every six hours, fixed.

684
00:44:25.082 --> 00:44:30.157
Snapshots expire after seven days, resolved
drifts are deleted thirty days after

685
00:44:30.157 --> 00:44:35.157
they're resolved, and updating a baseline
deletes that monitor's earlier results.

686
00:44:35.157 --> 00:44:37.576
Those are evidence rules in disguise:

687
00:44:37.576 --> 00:44:40.695
export every result before
changing a baseline.

688
00:44:40.695 --> 00:44:42.970
Every drift is one of two things.

689
00:44:42.970 --> 00:44:44.913
Unauthorized or accidental:

690
00:44:44.913 --> 00:44:49.608
redeploy the repository's version
and investigate from the audit log.

691
00:44:49.608 --> 00:44:53.821
Intended but not yet in code:
back-port it by pull request.

692
00:44:53.821 --> 00:44:58.168
A drift open more than one business
day goes to the governance office,

693
00:44:58.168 --> 00:45:01.660
and drift on a Tier 0 object
is a security incident.

694
00:45:02.410 --> 00:45:05.548
Configuration evidence
proves a setting existed.

695
00:45:05.548 --> 00:45:08.848
Operational evidence proves
the setting did its job.

696
00:45:08.848 --> 00:45:12.247
An assessor needs both, for
the whole assessment period,

697
00:45:12.247 --> 00:45:13.947
not just the day of the visit.

698
00:45:13.947 --> 00:45:15.961
The table is the program's mapping.

699
00:45:15.961 --> 00:45:20.917
AC-2, account management, is met by
access packages with approval and expiry,

700
00:45:20.917 --> 00:45:25.952
scheduled reviews, and Lifecycle Workflows;
the evidence is the package files,

701
00:45:25.952 --> 00:45:29.050
the entitlement audit
records for each request,

702
00:45:29.050 --> 00:45:32.148
the review history and
the workflow run history.

703
00:45:32.148 --> 00:45:37.147
AC-3, access enforcement, is the
Conditional Access library and group-based

704
00:45:37.147 --> 00:45:42.065
assignment, evidenced by policy files,
their deployment history, monitor

705
00:45:42.065 --> 00:45:47.386
results showing no unresolved drift, and
sign-in logs showing policies applied.

706
00:45:47.386 --> 00:45:51.723
AC-5, separation of duties, is
the three-party group model,

707
00:45:51.723 --> 00:45:55.873
incompatible packages and
two-person pull requests.

708
00:45:55.873 --> 00:46:01.357
AC-6 and its review enhancement are PIM
eligibility instead of standing roles,

709
00:46:01.357 --> 00:46:05.323
with reviews monthly for Tier
0 and quarterly otherwise.

710
00:46:05.323 --> 00:46:09.419
IA-2's MFA enhancements are the
phishing-resistant strength,

711
00:46:09.419 --> 00:46:13.435
shown in policy files, sign-ins
and the registration report.

712
00:46:13.435 --> 00:46:17.749
AU-11 isn't met by PIM configuration
at all, only by the export,

713
00:46:17.749 --> 00:46:23.560
and the validation test is an activation
still queryable after thirty-one days.

714
00:46:23.560 --> 00:46:27.564
And CA-7, continuous monitoring,
is the six-hourly monitor,

715
00:46:27.564 --> 00:46:32.335
the daily comparisons and the drift
issues with their resolutions.

716
00:46:32.335 --> 00:46:36.678
FedRAMP doesn't set the account-review
or privilege-review frequencies;

717
00:46:36.678 --> 00:46:41.673
they stay organization-defined, so the
security plan states the program's schedule.

718
00:46:41.673 --> 00:46:46.113
Under FedRAMP 20x, the same records
support the identity indicators,

719
00:46:46.113 --> 00:46:49.244
for automated account
management, least privilege,

720
00:46:49.244 --> 00:46:52.957
just-in-time authorization and
passwordless methods, and the

721
00:46:52.957 --> 00:46:57.835
change-management indicators for logging,
redeploying and validating changes.

722
00:46:57.835 --> 00:46:59.297
And the honest limits.

723
00:46:59.297 --> 00:47:02.642
A correct policy file says
nothing about whether reviewers

724
00:47:02.642 --> 00:47:05.785
read what they approved; the
review history carries that.

725
00:47:05.785 --> 00:47:07.648
Native retention is short.

726
00:47:07.648 --> 00:47:12.122
And tool coverage moves, so re-check
the coverage table at every upgrade.

727
00:47:12.122 --> 00:47:16.050
This is a design-intent mapping,
not an assessment result.

728
00:47:16.800 --> 00:47:19.563
To close, here's what to
take back to the tenant.

729
00:47:19.563 --> 00:47:23.454
The library: build the prerequisites
before the first policy,

730
00:47:23.454 --> 00:47:26.353
measure readiness rather
than estimating it,

731
00:47:26.353 --> 00:47:30.474
create every policy in report-only,
and enforce the waves in order.

732
00:47:30.474 --> 00:47:35.358
Exceptions: every exclusion is a governed
group with an owner and an expiry,

733
00:47:35.358 --> 00:47:40.713
and the break-glass accounts are alerted
on every use and tested every ninety days.

734
00:47:40.713 --> 00:47:44.819
PIM: emergency accounts first,
then the c1 policy switched On,

735
00:47:44.819 --> 00:47:49.912
then named approver groups, with every
human administrator eligible only.

736
00:47:49.912 --> 00:47:52.627
Governance: packages that always expire,

737
00:47:52.627 --> 00:47:57.176
reviews of groups rather than
applications, and auto-apply turned on.

738
00:47:57.176 --> 00:48:00.782
Code: one writer per object
class, two people per change,

739
00:48:00.782 --> 00:48:03.587
and drift monitored against the baseline.

740
00:48:03.587 --> 00:48:08.155
And evidence: exported before
Microsoft's thirty-day window closes,

741
00:48:08.155 --> 00:48:09.949
and assembled every month.

742
00:48:09.949 --> 00:48:14.250
In Zero Trust, every access
decision is a policy decision.

743
00:48:14.250 --> 00:48:17.708
This session has been about
making those policies explicit,

744
00:48:17.708 --> 00:48:21.650
testing them before they enforce,
and keeping the proof that they ran.

745
00:48:21.650 --> 00:48:24.741
Keep Appendix A at hand for
every policy's template,

746
00:48:24.741 --> 00:48:28.863
Appendix B for every role's settings,
and the Federal Modernization

747
00:48:28.863 --> 00:48:33.500
Workbook's Book 05 for the device
compliance that Conditional Access reads.

748
00:48:33.500 --> 00:48:34.320
Thank you.
